# Key Design Decisions

Several decisions significantly influenced the direction of the project.

## WordPress Instead of a Custom Framework

Building a custom framework would have demonstrated programming ability but contributed relatively little toward the overall objectives.

Using WordPress allowed more effort to be invested in engineering practices rather than recreating solved problems.

---

## Shibumi

Minimalism was adopted not as an aesthetic preference but as an engineering principle.

Visual simplicity supports clarity.

Clarity improves communication.

---

## Documentation-First Portfolio

Rather than hiding documentation behind repositories, documentation became one of the primary deliverables of the project.

The Engineering Handbook, project journals, ADRs, and GitHub repositories together communicate engineering thinking more effectively than screenshots alone.

---

## AI as a Collaborative Tool

AI was intentionally incorporated throughout planning, writing, design discussions, and architecture reviews.

Final decisions remained human decisions.

AI served as a collaborative reviewer rather than an autonomous creator.

---

# Challenges

Several challenges shaped the project.

* Defining what a professional portfolio should communicate.
* Balancing simplicity with sufficient technical depth.
* Organizing documentation across multiple repositories.
* Avoiding unnecessary complexity while still demonstrating engineering ability.
* Ensuring that the portfolio reflects long-term professional growth rather than a snapshot in time.

Each challenge reinforced the importance of thoughtful planning before implementation.

---

# Quality Assurance

Quality Assurance is treated as part of the project itself rather than merely a topic discussed within it.

The website serves as the first real-world application of my developing QA process.

Planned activities include:

* smoke testing,
* regression testing,
* exploratory testing,
* accessibility review,
* cross-browser verification,
* mobile responsiveness,
* and documentation review.

---

# AI Collaboration

AI supported the project by assisting with:

* architecture discussions,
* documentation refinement,
* engineering reviews,
* content organization,
* identifying inconsistencies,
* brainstorming,
* and challenging assumptions.

Throughout the project, AI functioned as a collaborative engineering partner while engineering judgment remained the responsibility of the author.

---

# Outcomes

Although the project remains under active development, it has already established several important foundations.

* A consistent engineering philosophy.
* A documentation-first workflow.
* A professional GitHub ecosystem.
* A repeatable project structure.
* A long-term engineering handbook.
* A portfolio centered on engineering judgment rather than technology lists.

---

# Lessons Learned

Several lessons continue to influence later work.

* Understanding the problem simplifies implementation.
* Simplicity requires discipline.
* Documentation improves engineering.
* Small iterations outperform large redesigns.
* AI is most valuable when it strengthens understanding.
* Quality should influence every stage of development.

